solution:
We have the equation $\frac{2x-1}{2x+2}=\frac{x-3}{x-1}$. Cross-multiplying and simplifying, we get \begin{align*}
(2x-1)(x-1)&=(2x+2)(x-3)\\
2x^2 - x - 2x + 1 &= 2x^2 + 2x - 3 \cdot 2x - 3 \cdot 2 \\
2x^2 - 3x + 1&=2x^2-4x-6\\
x&=\boxed{-7}
\end{align*}

We can rephrase the problem as the system of equations: \begin{align*}
\frac{a+2b}{2} &= 7\\
\frac{a+2c}{2} &= 8
\end{align*} Adding these gives: \begin{align*}
\frac{a+2b}{2}+\frac{a+2c}{2} &= 7+8\\
\frac{2a+2b+2c}{2} &= 15\\
a+b+c &= 15\\
\frac{a+b+c}{3} &= \frac{15}{3} = \boxed{5}
\end{align*}

